Staged scenes on disposable saves, recorded from two game copies on one PC.

[size=5][b]Install[/b][/size]
[b]One click:[/b] download the Main file, extract the zip anywhere, close KOTOR, and run [b]kotor-coop-...-setup.exe[/b]. It finds your GOG or Steam copy, lets you pick the folder if it cannot, and installs. Say yes to the firewall question if you want to host. This is not a Vortex-style drop-in mod, so install it with the setup program rather than through a mod manager.

[b]Prefer no installer?[/b] The GitHub releases page also has a plain Windows zip: extract it into its [i]own[/i] folder (not over the game) and run [b]KotorCoop.Agent.exe[/b]. It does the same job.

Then start KOTOR and press [b]Co-op[/b] on the main menu: [b]Host[/b], or pick your friend's server and [b]Connect[/b]. Over the internet, use a tunnel program such as playit.gg (there is no relay server).

[b]Both players need the same mod version.[/b] If another mod already uses [i]dinput8.dll[/i] (ReShade, an ASI loader and similar) the installer detects it and sets the two up side by side. To remove the mod, use the uninstaller in Windows "Installed apps", or run [i]KotorCoop.Agent.exe uninstall[/i].

[size=5][b]What to expect[/b][/size]
[list]
[*]This is a hobby experiment released as open source. [b]A complete two-player run from start to finish has not been played yet.[/b] The early game and selected later scenes have been tried on two real copies; expect rough edges.
[*]One conversation at a time: whoever starts it chooses the replies.
[*]The host leads the party. Minigames (Pazaak, swoop racing, turrets) are single-seat.
[*]The game runs at its own 800x600 size in co-op; wide-screen resolutions fell back to 800x600 when tried.
[*]The files are not code-signed, so Windows SmartScreen may warn ("More info", then "Run anyway"). Compare the SHA-256 on the GitHub release page if you want to check. Please report antivirus detections rather than adding exclusions.
[/list]
The honest, area-by-area list is in the project's limitations page (linked below).

[size=5][b]How it works, and other co-op mods[/b][/size]
This began as an experiment in how far a 2003 game can be taken purely by scanning and hooking its memory; the mod is a side effect. The game executable on disk is never changed. A small plugin loaded as [i]dinput8.dll[/i] hooks the running game, and an external helper talks to the other players. There are other, more recent KOTOR co-op projects built on different approaches (a dedicated server; an engine re-implementation), and they may well do better in the long run. This one is open source (GPL-3.0), so take whatever is useful.

